      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0221",
      "sequence": 233,
      "meaning": "‘Tell me, friend, what embodied being would not be enchanted on seeing this beauty?’ One woman lovingly replied in a gentle voice, ‘Wise friends, hear what I have heard. These two are Daśaratha’s sons, a charming pair of young swans. They guarded sage Kauśika’s sacrifice and killed the night-rangers in the battlefield. The dark-bodied one with lovely lotus-eyes, who broke Mārīca’s and Subāhu’s pride, is Kauśalyā’s son, a mine of joy; his name is Rāma, and he bears bow and arrows. The fair youth in the fine warrior’s dress, walking behind Rāma with bow and arrow, is Lakṣmaṇa, Rāma’s younger brother; Sumitrā is his mother. Having accomplished the Brahmins’ work and delivered the sage’s wife on the road, the brothers have come after hearing of the bow-sacrifice.’ All the women rejoiced.",
